\description{
Read a FlashLFQ QuantifiedPeptides.tsv (or MetaMorpheus AllQuantifiedPeptides.tsv) as one row per peptide per sample, with the sample's intensity and detection type as columns.

Reads FlashLFQ's \code{QuantifiedPeptides.tsv}, or the \code{AllQuantifiedPeptides.tsv} MetaMorpheus
writes with it, long: one row per peptide per sample, with that sample's intensity and detection
type as columns.
}
\details{
\strong{An intensity of 0 is not a measurement.} FlashLFQ writes a literal 0 for a peptide it did not
quantify in a sample, and mzLib reads it as 0; \code{detection_type} - \code{"MSMS"}, \code{"MBR"},
\code{"NotDetected"} and so on - is what tells the two apart.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_quantified_peptide_records}. \code{record_count} counts the peptides in the whole
  file; \code{returned_count} the peptides returned, starting \code{offset} peptides in; \code{row_count} the
  rows in \code{records}, peptides times samples. \code{sample_labels} are the file's sample labels in
  header order.

  \code{records} has one row per peptide per sample: \code{intensity} in the instrument's intensity units
  (0 is not a measurement - see above); \code{detection_type}; and \code{retention_time} in minutes,
  written only by IsoTracker. For any other file \code{retention_time} and \code{peak_order} are \code{NA} in
  every row and \code{absent_fields} names them. \code{rows_not_read} is \code{NA}: every line is a peptide, or
  the file fails to read.

}
